### How do you implement the CheckedFunction interface?

```java
package com.bank.util;

@FunctionalInterface
public interface CheckedFunction<T, R> {
    R apply(T t) throws Exception;
}
```

This interface is identical to `Function<T, R>` except that its `apply` method declares `throws Exception`, so a lambda that throws a checked exception can satisfy it.

### How do you implement the unchecked() wrapper?

```java
package com.bank.util;

import java.util.function.Function;

public final class StreamUtils {

    private StreamUtils() {}  // utility class

    public static <T, R> Function<T, R> unchecked(CheckedFunction<T, R> f) {
        return t -> {
            try {
                return f.apply(t);
            } catch (Exception e) {
                throw new RuntimeException(e);  // preserves cause
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### How do you use it in a stream?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import static com.bank.util.StreamUtils.unchecked;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.text.SimpleDateFormat;
import java.util.Date;
import java.util.List;

public class UncheckedUsage {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> dateStrings = List.of("2024-01-15", "2024-06-30");

        List<Date> dates = dateStrings.stream()
            .map(unchecked(s -> new SimpleDateFormat("yyyy-MM-dd").parse(s)))
            .toList();

        dates.forEach(d -> System.out.println("Parsed: " + d));
    }
}
```

```text
Parsed: Mon Jan 15 00:00:00 IST 2024
Parsed: Sun Jun 30 00:00:00 IST 2024
```

### How do you extend it for Consumer and Supplier?

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface CheckedConsumer<T> {
    void accept(T t) throws Exception;
}

@FunctionalInterface
interface CheckedSupplier<T> {
    T get() throws Exception;
}

public static <T> java.util.function.Consumer<T> unchecked(CheckedConsumer<T> c) {
    return t -> {
        try { c.accept(t); }
        catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    };
}

public static <T> java.util.function.Supplier<T> unchecked(CheckedSupplier<T> s) {
    return () -> {
        try { return s.get(); }
        catch (Exception e) { throw new RuntimeException(e); }
    };
}
```

### What about specific exception types?

```java
// For IOException, use UncheckedIOException for clarity
import java.io.UncheckedIOException;
import java.io.IOException;

public static <T, R> Function<T, R> uncheckedIO(CheckedFunction<T, R> f) {
    return t -> {
        try {
            return f.apply(t);
        } catch (IOException e) {
            throw new UncheckedIOException(e);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    };
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: Not preserving the cause in the rethrown exception.

```java
// WRONG: cause lost, stack trace incomplete
catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeException("failed");
}

// CORRECT: cause preserved
catch (Exception e) {
    throw new RuntimeException(e);
}
```

### How is this different from Lombok's @SneakyThrows?

| Approach | Mechanism | Safety |
|---|---|---|
| `unchecked()` wrapper | Catches and rethrows as unchecked | Explicit, type-safe |
| `@SneakyThrows` (Lombok) | Tricks the compiler into allowing checked throws | Implicit, bypasses type system |
| Inline try-catch | Manual in every lambda | Verbose but explicit |

### What should a senior engineer know?

The wrapper centralizes the checked-to-unchecked conversion so it is written once and used everywhere. Always preserve the cause, and prefer specific unchecked types (`UncheckedIOException`) over bare `RuntimeException`. For expected failures, prefer result-objects over exceptions.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why not use @SneakyThrows?" It bypasses the type system and surprises callers who do not expect checked exceptions.
- "Can you make it work with Predicate?" Yes, create a CheckedPredicate interface and an unchecked wrapper.
- "Why preserve the cause?" So the original exception and stack trace are visible in logs and debuggers.
